    "intro": "Satyrs are the goat-legged spirits of the Greek wilds, companions of the wine god and famous for music and dancing; the Romans called their gentler cousins fauns. This **satyr name generator** makes Greek-sounding names, woodland names like Ivyhorn, and names for D&D satyr bards.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Satyrs in Greek myth",
        "content": "<p>Satyrs were the wild followers of Dionysus, god of wine, known for music, dancing and causing trouble.</p><ul><li><strong>Horse, then goat:</strong> in early Greek art satyrs have horses' ears and tails. The goat legs came later, as they merged with Pan and the Roman fauns.</li><li><strong>Satyr plays:</strong> Athenian playwrights followed three tragedies with a rude, comic satyr play. Only one survives complete, Euripides's <em>Cyclops</em>.</li><li><strong>The pipes:</strong> the syrinx, or panpipe, is the satyr's instrument, named after a nymph who was turned into reeds.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Naming a satyr",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Greek</strong> names come from wine, dance and revelry: Komastes, \"reveller\", with a home \"of Arcadia\".</li><li><strong>Woodland</strong> names are all ivy and horn: Ivyhorn, Hazeltail, Acornhorn.</li><li><strong>D&amp;D bards:</strong> satyrs make natural bards, so this style gives them a stage name like Pip Merrypipe.</li></ul><p>For the nymphs of the same woods, try the <a href=\"nymph-name-generator.html\">Nymph Name Generator</a>.</p><p>For more beasts of legend, see all the <a href=\"../categories/mythical-names.html\">mythical creature name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
